# Code Quality

## No Comments in Code

- Code must be self-explanatory through clear naming, small scopes, and obvious control flow
- Replace comments with better function names, variables, or helpers
- Record rationale in `docs/technical_decisions.md`, not source files

## Linting and Formatting

- Formatter and linter must be run automatically on every change
- Fix all warnings and errors. Never disable rules unless documented in `technical_decisions.md`

## Strict Typing

- Use precise types, generics, and discriminated unions. Never use `any` or weak types
- Prefer type guards and exhaustiveness checks
- Public APIs must always have full types

## No Code Bloat

- Code should be lean and purposeful. Remove obsolete code promptly
- Avoid speculative abstractions. Keep dependencies minimal and justified

## No Duplication of Logic

- Extract shared utilities or components for similar behavior
- Favor DRY across modules, styles, and config
- Refactor toward a single source of truth

## No Unnecessary Files

- Do not add placeholder files or throwaway debug artifacts
- Temporary helpers must be removed immediately after use

## Single Responsibility and Clear Boundaries

- Ensure each module/component does one thing well
- Separate domain logic, presentation, configuration, and side-effects

## Explicit Error Handling

- Handle errors explicitly where they occur; return useful error data to callers
- Avoid silent failures. Validate all inputs. Keep error messages concise

## Performance by Design

- Avoid unnecessary renders or allocations. Use efficient data structures. Cache if appropriate
- Measure before further optimizations
